@section('path')/docs/context@endsection
@section('description')Each Propel view has one Rust context struct, filled by its handler. The view reads the struct's fields by name and cannot use a field it lacks.@endsection
@section('og_type')article@endsection
@section('structured_data')<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"WebPage","name":"Context and composers","url":"https://propel.rocketboost.rs/docs/context","description":"Each Propel view has one Rust context struct, filled by its handler. The view reads the struct's fields by name and cannot use a field it lacks."}</script>@endsection

@section('title')Context and composers@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>Context and composers</h1>
  <p>Every view has one context: a Rust struct that holds the data the view needs. The handler builds it, and the view reads its fields. A view cannot use a field that the struct does not have.</p>

  <h2>Define the context</h2>
  <p>Derive <code>Serialize</code> so Propel can read the fields.</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">#[derive(Serialize)]
pub struct PostsIndex {
    pub title: String,
    pub posts: Vec&lt;Post&gt;,
}</code></pre>

  <h2>Build it in a handler</h2>
  <p>The route is the view composer for this page: it collects the data and passes it to the view.</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">#[get("/posts")]
fn posts() -&gt; PropelView {
    PropelView::render("posts.index", &amp;PostsIndex {
        title: "Posts".into(),
        posts: all_posts(),
    })
}</code></pre>

  <h2>Read it in the view</h2>
  <p>Fields are read by name, as in <code>$title</code> or <code>$post.title</code>. The <code>$</code> is optional, and a loop variable such as <code>post</code> is read the same way.</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@extends('layouts.app')

@@section('content')
  &lt;h1&gt;@{{ $title }}&lt;/h1&gt;
  @@forelse (post in posts)
    &lt;h2&gt;@{{ $post.title }}&lt;/h2&gt;
  @@empty
    &lt;p&gt;No posts yet.&lt;/p&gt;
  @@endforelse
@@endsection</code></pre>

  <h2>Shared data with <code>Page</code></h2>
  <p>Some data is needed on every page, such as the sidebar and the previous and next links. The test app wraps each context in a <code>Page</code>, which adds that data next to the view's own fields.</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">PropelView::render("posts.index", &amp;Page::new("/posts", PostsIndex {
    title: "Posts".into(),
    posts: all_posts(),
}))</code></pre>
  <p>The layout then reads the shared data like any other field:</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@foreach (group in nav)
  &lt;p&gt;@{{ $group.heading }}&lt;/p&gt;
  @@foreach (link in group.links)
    &lt;a href="@{{ $link.href }}"&gt;@{{ $link.label }}&lt;/a&gt;
  @@endforeach
@@endforeach</code></pre>

  <h2>Status</h2>
  <p>The <code>Page</code> wrapper is part of the test app for now, not of the library. In Laravel terms, it is a view composer written by hand. Propel will offer a request guard for shared data later. A view composer that runs on every request is planned, but not built.</p>
@endsection
